{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "At fixed masses and center-of-mass speed, the reduction from the momentum-average post-collision velocity depends on relative speed through one smooth monotone saturating shape set only by mass ratio and normalized approach speed.",
      "child_relation": "Retain the same momentum-conserving average minus a dissipative correction, but make the correction's dependence on relative speed sharpen disproportionately in nearly head-on, low-center-of-mass-speed encounters by replacing the parent's single smooth seating profile with a more abrupt symmetry-weighted seating transition.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "m1",
        "m2",
        "u1",
        "u2"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "Holding m1,m2 and the momentum-average speed fixed, increasing |u1-u2| produces a mild smooth drop in v_post in the parent, but a distinctly steeper mid-range drop before saturation in the child when masses are near equal and the collision is close to head-on."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "vcm",
        "expression": "(m1*u1 + m2*u2)/(m1 + m2)",
        "role": "incoming center-of-mass translational speed setting the normalization scale"
      },
      {
        "name": "rel",
        "expression": "u1 - u2",
        "role": "signed approach speed controlling impact severity"
      },
      {
        "name": "sym",
        "expression": "4*m1*m2/(m1 + m2)**2",
        "role": "mass-symmetry factor, maximal for equal masses and small for strong mass asymmetry"
      },
      {
        "name": "chi",
        "expression": "((u1 - u2)/((m1*u1 + m2*u2)/(m1 + m2)))**2",
        "role": "normalized head-on severity already implicit in the parent response"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when the carts approach with non-negligible relative speed and small to moderate |vcm| so chi is order one or larger, especially for sym near 1 (m1≈m2); weak in strongly unequal-mass impacts or when |rel| << |vcm|.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "Within training-like variations of m1,m2,u1,u2, the same symmetry-weighted severity coordinate already changes; in holdout regions with more equal masses and more nearly opposing incoming velocities, the child extends this into a sharper mid-range curvature change rather than a uniform gain change.",
    "continuation_path": "In the limit that the sharpened seating transition is expanded back to the parent's smoother first-order saturating profile, or in regimes chi<<1 or sym->0, the child reduces effectively to the current law.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The current model already includes symmetry-dependent internal/rotational participation, but it assumes a single smooth saturation shape for latch seating across all admissible severities; it omits the possibility of a sharper crossover from mostly translational sticking to stronger internal participation in nearly symmetric head-on impacts.",
    "baseline_assumption": "Relax the parent's implicit assumption that the seating/deformation contribution grows with one fixed smooth tanh-like profile once expressed in the existing normalized severity variable.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Abrupt latch seating/compliance engagement: once relative slip and compression exceed a modest normalized level, internal deformation/rotational channels capture translational energy more suddenly, with the onset amplified for symmetric cart pairings.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "m1",
      "m2",
      "u1",
      "u2"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to change the mathematical form of the already-present dissipative participation mechanism, not add a new process. The parent target mapping is shortcut-like because one shared smooth saturating profile governs all admissible collision severities after normalization. Vary |u1-u2| while holding m1,m2 and vcm=(m1*u1+m2*u2)/(m1+m2) fixed. In the parent, v_post decreases with a fixed smooth curvature as normalized severity chi grows. In the child, the decrease remains monotone, bounded between incoming velocities, momentum-conserving, and dissipative, but the slope in the mid-range becomes noticeably steeper before saturating when sym=4 m1 m2/(m1+m2)^2 is near 1. That is a changed scored target mapping, not merely an auxiliary reinterpretation, because the predicted v_post-versus-input surface bends differently across the same supplied inputs. This effect is scientifically admissible for nearly equal masses and near-opposite incoming velocities where latch seating and deformation can switch on more abruptly; it is limited and may be hard to detect when masses are very unequal or the relative speed is small compared with the center-of-mass speed. Refitting the parent's existing shape globally cannot reproduce both a gentle response in asymmetric impacts and a sharper mid-range drop in symmetric head-on impacts if one shared smooth profile must serve all conditions."
  },
